## Why it exists

**ASP.NET Core** is Microsoft's open-source web framework for .NET. It is what most C# web APIs, websites and back-end services run on, and it is consistently among the faster mainstream frameworks. It gives you what Express gave you in the previous lesson (a pipeline of middleware and routes) and adds the things Express leaves to you: **built-in dependency injection**, **typed configuration**, **model binding and validation**, **authentication and authorisation**, **rate limiting**, **health checks**, **OpenAPI** support and a first-class **testing** story.

There are two styles for writing endpoints. **Controllers** (classes with attributes, the older MVC style) suit large applications with many conventions. **Minimal APIs** (a few lines in `Program.cs`, the style this lesson uses) are the modern, low-ceremony default for new services and are what the current templates generate. Both run on the same pipeline.

**Important:** like the previous lesson, **nothing here was compiled or run**: there is no .NET SDK in the sandbox. The code uses long-stable minimal-API features whose shapes were checked against Microsoft's documentation, and it deliberately avoids the newest features whose exact syntax I could not confirm. Run it with `dotnet run` and treat any compiler message as authoritative over this text.

**Mental model:** the same **assembly line** as Express. A request enters through the **Kestrel** web server, passes through a **pipeline of middleware** (each one can respond, pass on, or fail), reaches an **endpoint** (your lambda), and the response travels back out through the same middleware in reverse. The new idea is a **service container** next to the pipeline: you register services once, and the framework **injects** them into endpoints by parameter type.

{{dg:0}}

## How it works

### The smallest API

`dotnet new web -o NotesApi` creates a minimal project. Its entire program is a handful of lines (the shape shown in Microsoft's minimal-API documentation):

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);   // configuration, logging and the service container
var app = builder.Build();                          // freezes services, starts the pipeline definition

app.MapGet("/", () => "Hello World!");              // an endpoint: method + path + handler

app.Run();                                          // start Kestrel and wait
```

Run it with `dotnet run`; the console prints the local URL. Two phases matter: **before `Build()` you register services** (`builder.Services...`), and **after it you configure the pipeline and endpoints** (`app.Use...`, `app.Map...`). Mixing them up is a common beginner error.

### A notes API (the same one from the HTTP lesson)

Below is the notes service from the HTTP lesson, written as a minimal API with a service behind an interface, typed results, validation and a route group. Put it in `Program.cs`:

```csharp
using System.Collections.Concurrent;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http.HttpResults;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddSingleton<INoteStore, InMemoryNoteStore>();   // register: interface -> implementation
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();                              // standard JSON error bodies (RFC 9457 style)
builder.Services.AddOpenApi();                                     // OpenAPI document generation

var app = builder.Build();

app.UseExceptionHandler();                                         // turn unhandled exceptions into 500 problem details
app.UseStatusCodePages();                                          // give bare 404s etc. a problem-details body

if (app.Environment.IsDevelopment())
    app.MapOpenApi();                                              // serves the generated OpenAPI JSON

var notes = app.MapGroup("/notes");                                // shared prefix for these endpoints

notes.MapGet("/", (INoteStore store, int? limit) =>                // INoteStore comes from DI, limit from ?limit=
    TypedResults.Ok(store.List(limit)));

notes.MapGet("/{id:int}", Results<Ok<Note>, NotFound> (int id, INoteStore store) =>
    store.Find(id) is { } note ? TypedResults.Ok(note) : TypedResults.NotFound());

notes.MapPost("/", Results<Created<Note>, ValidationProblem> (CreateNote request, INoteStore store) =>
{
    if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(request.Text))
        return TypedResults.ValidationProblem(new Dictionary<string, string[]>
        {
            ["text"] = ["Field 'text' must be a non-empty string."],
        });

    var note = store.Add(request.Text.Trim());
    return TypedResults.Created($"/notes/{note.Id}", note);        // 201 + Location header
});

notes.MapDelete("/{id:int}", Results<NoContent, NotFound> (int id, INoteStore store) =>
    store.Remove(id) ? TypedResults.NoContent() : TypedResults.NotFound());

app.Run();

public record Note(int Id, string Text);
public record CreateNote(string Text);

public interface INoteStore
{
    IReadOnlyList<Note> List(int? limit);
    Note? Find(int id);
    Note Add(string text);
    bool Remove(int id);
}

public class InMemoryNoteStore : INoteStore
{
    private readonly ConcurrentDictionary<int, Note> _notes = new();
    private int _nextId;

    public IReadOnlyList<Note> List(int? limit) =>
        _notes.Values.OrderBy(n => n.Id).Take(limit ?? int.MaxValue).ToList();

    public Note? Find(int id) => _notes.GetValueOrDefault(id);

    public Note Add(string text)
    {
        var note = new Note(Interlocked.Increment(ref _nextId), text);
        _notes[note.Id] = note;
        return note;
    }

    public bool Remove(int id) => _notes.TryRemove(id, out _);
}
```

Walk through what the framework does for you, because this is where ASP.NET Core differs from Express:

- **Model binding.** The framework fills handler parameters **by type and name**: `int id` from the route, `int? limit` from the query string, `CreateNote request` from the JSON body (complex types bind from the body by default in minimal APIs), and `INoteStore store` from the **service container**. You write no parsing code, and a request that cannot be bound (for example `id` not an integer, which the `{id:int}` constraint rejects) never reaches your lambda.
- **Dependency injection.** `AddSingleton` registers one shared instance for the whole application. The other lifetimes are `AddScoped` (one per request, the right choice for a database context) and `AddTransient` (a new one each time). Endpoints depend on the **interface**, so tests can substitute a fake, exactly the technique from the Node testing lesson but built into the framework. A **singleton** that holds state must be **thread-safe** (hence `ConcurrentDictionary` and `Interlocked`), because requests run in parallel.
- **Typed results.** Returning `TypedResults.Ok(...)`, `NotFound()`, `Created(...)` and `NoContent()` produces the status codes from the HTTP lesson, and declaring the union `Results<Ok<Note>, NotFound>` makes the possible responses part of the method's **type**, which also feeds the OpenAPI document and unit tests. `Results.Ok(...)` (without `Typed`) works too but loses that information.
- **Validation.** Here it is explicit (`string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace` and a `ValidationProblem` that returns **400** with a standard problem-details body listing field errors). Recent ASP.NET Core versions also added built-in validation for minimal APIs driven by data-annotation attributes; check the documentation for your version rather than relying on this lesson for its exact registration.
- **Problem details.** `AddProblemDetails`, `UseExceptionHandler` and `UseStatusCodePages` make all errors share one JSON shape (the "problem details" standard), instead of every endpoint inventing its own.
- **Route groups** (`MapGroup`) share a prefix, filters, authorisation and metadata across endpoints.

Expected behaviour (reasoned from the code and the HTTP lesson, **not run**): `POST /notes` with `{"text":"hello"}` returns `201` with a `Location: /notes/1` header; `GET /notes/99` returns `404`; a body without `text` returns `400` with a problem-details JSON; `DELETE /notes/1` returns `204`, and a second delete returns `404`.

### The middleware pipeline

The order of `app.Use...` calls **is** the order of execution, just as in the Express lesson, and ASP.NET Core's documentation gives a recommended order. A typical production pipeline reads, top to bottom: exception handling, HTTPS redirection and HSTS, static files, routing, CORS, authentication, authorisation, rate limiting, then endpoints. Writing your own middleware uses the same "do something, call next, do something after" shape:

```csharp
app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    var started = DateTime.UtcNow;
    await next(context);                               // everything after this point runs on the way back
    var ms = (DateTime.UtcNow - started).TotalMilliseconds;
    app.Logger.LogInformation("{Method} {Path} -> {Status} in {Ms:F0} ms",
        context.Request.Method, context.Request.Path, context.Response.StatusCode, ms);
});
```

### Configuration and secrets

`appsettings.json` holds defaults, `appsettings.Development.json` overrides them locally, **environment variables** override both (the way containers and cloud hosts pass settings), and command-line arguments override everything. Bind a section to a typed class with the options pattern (`builder.Services.Configure<MySettings>(builder.Configuration.GetSection("My"))`) and inject `IOptions<MySettings>`. For local secrets use `dotnet user-secrets init` and `dotnet user-secrets set "Db:Password" "..."`, which keeps them out of the repository; in production use environment variables or a vault such as Azure Key Vault. The environment name (`Development`, `Staging`, `Production`) comes from `ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT`.

### Data with Entity Framework Core

**EF Core** is the standard object-relational mapper: you define classes and a `DbContext`, query with LINQ, and EF generates **parameterised SQL**. Register the context with `builder.Services.AddDbContext<AppDb>(options => options.UseNpgsql(connectionString))` (the provider package and method depend on the database: PostgreSQL, SQL Server, SQLite), and inject `AppDb` into endpoints. A `DbContext` is **scoped** (one per request) and not thread-safe. Schema changes are **migrations** (`dotnet ef migrations add AddNotes`, `dotnet ef database update`), kept in version control and applied by a deployment step, not by the web app at start-up in production. Watch for **N+1 queries** (use `Include` or projections), and use `AsNoTracking()` for read-only queries. Microsoft's minimal-API tutorial uses the in-memory provider for demos; do not use it to test real database behaviour.

### Authentication, authorisation and other built-ins

- **Authentication** (who are you?) is added with `builder.Services.AddAuthentication().AddJwtBearer(...)` for token-based APIs (or cookies for web apps, or an external identity provider such as Microsoft Entra ID), and `app.UseAuthentication()`.
- **Authorisation** (may you?) uses `app.UseAuthorization()` and per-endpoint rules: `notes.MapPost(...).RequireAuthorization()` or policies (`RequireAuthorization("AdminOnly")`). Check ownership in the handler as the web-security lesson explained: a policy says you may call the endpoint, not that you may touch *this* note.
- **Rate limiting** is built in (`AddRateLimiter` and `UseRateLimiter`), as are **CORS** (`AddCors`, `UseCors` with an exact origin allow-list, per the browser-security lesson), **health checks** (`AddHealthChecks`, `MapHealthChecks("/health")`), **response compression**, **output caching** and **HTTP/2**.

### Testing

The framework ships a test host: `WebApplicationFactory<Program>` (from the `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing` package) starts your whole application in memory and gives you an `HttpClient`, so tests exercise routing, DI, middleware and serialisation with no real network. Because the program uses top-level statements, expose the entry point to tests by adding `public partial class Program { }` at the bottom of `Program.cs`. Override services for a test (a fake store, an in-memory database) with `WithWebHostBuilder`.

```csharp
// NotesApi.Tests (xUnit). Not run in this lesson's sandbox.
public class NotesApiTests : IClassFixture<WebApplicationFactory<Program>>
{
    private readonly HttpClient _client;
    public NotesApiTests(WebApplicationFactory<Program> factory) => _client = factory.CreateClient();

    [Fact]
    public async Task Post_creates_a_note_and_returns_201_with_location()
    {
        var response = await _client.PostAsJsonAsync("/notes", new { text = "hello" });

        Assert.Equal(HttpStatusCode.Created, response.StatusCode);
        Assert.Equal("/notes/1", response.Headers.Location?.ToString());
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task Get_unknown_note_returns_404()
    {
        var response = await _client.GetAsync("/notes/999");
        Assert.Equal(HttpStatusCode.NotFound, response.StatusCode);
    }
}
```

## Worked example

Goal: take the notes API from a demo to something you could deploy. Work through this list in order; each step names the section above that supports it.

1. **Replace the in-memory store** with EF Core and PostgreSQL: a `Note` entity, an `AppDb` context registered with `AddDbContext`, migrations in the repository, and the connection string from an environment variable (never from source control).
2. **Make notes belong to users.** Add JWT bearer authentication, `RequireAuthorization()` on the group, and an `OwnerId` column; every query filters by the current user's id taken from the token's claims. Return `404` for another user's note (the IDOR lesson).
3. **Validate properly:** maximum text length, trimming, and a request body size limit; keep returning `ValidationProblem` so clients get one error shape.
4. **Harden the pipeline:** HTTPS redirection and HSTS, an exact-origin CORS policy if a browser app calls it, a rate limiter on write endpoints, security headers, and the exception handler configured to hide internals in production.
5. **Operate it:** structured logging, a `/health` endpoint, OpenAPI published for client teams, and an OpenTelemetry or Application Insights setup so latency and errors are visible.
6. **Test it:** unit tests for the store, `WebApplicationFactory` tests for each endpoint including `401`, `403`, `404`, `400`, plus a cross-user access test that must fail.
7. **Ship it:** `dotnet publish -c Release`, built in a multi-stage Dockerfile that uses the official .NET SDK image to build and the ASP.NET runtime image to run, a non-root user, and a readiness probe pointing at `/health`.

The steps use different tools but the same ideas as the Express, security and testing lessons: a pipeline, injected dependencies, explicit trust boundaries and automated checks.

## In real products

- **Enterprise and SaaS back ends** run ASP.NET Core in containers on Azure App Service, Azure Container Apps, Kubernetes or AWS, behind a load balancer or API gateway, with Entra ID or another OpenID Connect provider for sign-in.
- **Typical structure:** a web project with endpoints (or controllers), a domain or application layer with the business rules, an infrastructure layer with EF Core and external clients, and test projects; large teams add background workers (`IHostedService` or `BackgroundService`) for queues and scheduled jobs.
- **Microservices and orchestration:** .NET Aspire provides a way to describe and run several services and their dependencies together, and gRPC is well supported for service-to-service calls (see the API lessons).
- **Observability:** OpenTelemetry is built into the platform's diagnostics, and Application Insights, Prometheus and Grafana, or Datadog collect the data.
- **Performance and cost:** Kestrel handles very high request rates on small machines, and options such as ahead-of-time compilation, response caching and output caching reduce cost further; as always, measure first.
- **Versioning in practice:** keep the framework on a supported release (see the C# lesson for the dates), keep packages updated, and run `dotnet list package --outdated --vulnerable` in CI.

> [!pitfall] Wrong service lifetime. Injecting a scoped service (such as a `DbContext`) into a singleton captures it forever and breaks under concurrency; a singleton holding mutable state without locking corrupts data under parallel requests. Use scoped for per-request state, singleton only for thread-safe or immutable services, and enable scope validation in development.

> [!pitfall] Middleware order mistakes: authorisation before authentication, CORS after the endpoint, exception handling not first, or `UseStatusCodePages` hiding useful information. The order of `app.Use...` calls is the execution order, so follow the documented recommendation and test with requests that should be rejected.

> [!pitfall] Doing blocking or slow work in an endpoint (`.Result`, synchronous database or file calls, long CPU loops). Server threads are limited, and a few blocked requests can stall the whole service. Use `async`/`await` end to end and a background queue for slow jobs.

> [!pitfall] Returning your database entities directly from endpoints. It leaks internal columns (password hashes, internal ids, navigation properties that cause serialisation loops) and ties the API to the schema. Return dedicated DTOs (records), and bind request bodies to input records, never to entities (mass assignment).

> [!tip] Keep `Program.cs` short by moving endpoints into extension methods (`app.MapNotes()`) and services into registration methods (`services.AddNotes()`). Use records for requests and responses, `TypedResults` for precise responses, one problem-details shape for all errors, and a strongly typed options class per configuration section validated at start-up.

> [!tip] Use the built-in diagnostics: `dotnet watch` for rapid feedback, the OpenAPI document and a client such as a REST file in your editor for manual calls, `app.Logger` with structured placeholders (not string concatenation) for logs, and `WebApplicationFactory` tests as the safety net for refactoring.

> [!cost] Costs come from the hosting plan, the database, bandwidth and monitoring. .NET services are often cheap to run because of their low memory and CPU per request, but chatty database access (N+1 queries), unbounded result sets without paging, and verbose logging can dominate the bill. Add paging, projections and caching, set log levels per environment, and size containers from measurements.

> [!security] The framework offers secure defaults but not secure applications. Enforce authentication and per-object authorisation; validate and limit input; use HTTPS with HSTS; configure CORS with exact origins and anti-forgery protection for cookie-based browser apps; use EF Core or parameterised SQL (never string-built SQL); keep secrets in user-secrets locally and a vault in production; rate-limit authentication endpoints; hide stack traces outside development; and keep the runtime and packages patched (scan with `dotnet list package --vulnerable`). Apply the same attack checklist as in the web-attacks and browser-security lessons: those vulnerabilities are language-independent.

> [!prod] Production checklist: current LTS runtime and patched base images; configuration from environment variables or a vault, with options validated at start-up; EF Core migrations applied by the deployment pipeline; authentication, authorisation and ownership checks tested; HTTPS, HSTS, CORS allow-list and rate limiting; problem-details errors with no internals; health and readiness endpoints; structured logs, metrics and traces; graceful shutdown; an automated build, test and container publish pipeline; and load testing before launch.

> [!version] Researched 2026-10-23 from Microsoft's documentation as returned by web search: the minimal API overview (the `WebApplication.CreateBuilder`, `MapGet` and `app.Run` structure, `TypedResults`), the minimal-API tutorial's use of EF Core's in-memory provider, and the project template's built-in OpenAPI hint (`AddOpenApi`, `MapOpenApi`); and from the .NET support policy for the LTS dates quoted in the C# lesson. NOT run: every line of C# and every `dotnet` command (there is no .NET SDK, compiler or network in the sandbox), so syntax, overload names and package names (`Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing`, `UseNpgsql` and others) come from my knowledge of the framework and were not compiled. Not confirmed from the documentation in this session: the recommended middleware order, `AddRateLimiter` details, the options pattern calls, the built-in minimal-API validation feature (deliberately not used), Dockerfile base-image names, .NET Aspire, and the linked resources, which could not be opened offline. Compile and test the examples before using them.

lang-overview.md:
## Why it exists

Python and TypeScript will take you a long way, and the rest of this Atlas leans on them. But you will meet other languages constantly: the Go service your platform team owns, the Rust library inside your tooling, the Java or C# estate at a bank or enterprise, the Kotlin Android app, the C underneath everything. Choosing among them (or just reading them) is easier when you compare on a handful of axes rather than on fashion: **how memory is managed, how concurrency works, how types behave, how code is built and shipped, and what ecosystem surrounds it**. The syntax is the easy part.

**Mental model:** every language is a bundle of trade-offs. Garbage-collected languages (Go, Java, C#, Python) trade some memory overhead and pauses for productivity and safety. Rust trades a steeper learning curve for memory safety and predictable performance without a garbage collector. C and C++ trade safety for maximum control. Dynamically typed languages trade early error detection for speed of writing. The right language is the one whose trade-offs fit the problem, the team and the ecosystem, which is why "the best language" is a question about context.

{{dg:0}}

## How it works

### The axes that matter

- **Memory management:** *garbage collection* (a runtime finds and frees unused objects: Go, Java, C#, Python, JavaScript), *ownership and borrowing* (the compiler proves memory is freed exactly once: Rust), *reference counting* (Swift, parts of Python), or *manual* (C, C++). This drives latency predictability, memory use and which bugs are possible (use-after-free, data races).
- **Typing:** static versus dynamic, strong versus weak, and how expressive the type system is (generics, sum types, null safety). Stronger static types move errors from production to compile time and make large refactors safer.
- **Concurrency model:** OS threads and locks, async/await on an event loop, lightweight green threads (Go goroutines, Java virtual threads), or message passing. See the OS concepts and async lessons for the underlying ideas.
- **Compilation and deployment:** interpreted, bytecode on a virtual machine (JVM, .NET CLR), or ahead-of-time compiled to a native binary. A single static binary (Go, Rust) is trivial to deploy; a JVM or .NET app needs a runtime or a bundled image.
- **Performance profile:** startup time, steady-state speed, memory footprint, and tail latency. Most business software is I/O-bound, where language speed matters far less than architecture, queries and caching.
- **Ecosystem and talent:** libraries, frameworks, tooling, documentation, hiring pool and long-term support. Often the deciding factor.

### Go: simple, concurrent, easy to deploy

Go (from Google, first released 2012) values a small language, fast compilation and straightforward concurrency. It is statically typed with a garbage collector and compiles to a **single static binary** with cross-compilation built in (`GOOS=linux GOARCH=arm64 go build`). Key features:

- **Goroutines and channels:** goroutines are very cheap concurrent functions (thousands to millions are fine), multiplexed by the runtime onto OS threads; channels pass data between them. `context.Context` carries cancellation and deadlines.
- **Errors are values:** functions return `(result, error)` and you handle `if err != nil` explicitly. Verbose, but flow is obvious and there are no hidden exceptions.
- **Interfaces are structural:** a type satisfies an interface by having the methods, with no `implements` keyword. **Generics** arrived in Go 1.18.
- **Batteries included:** a strong standard library (`net/http`, `encoding/json`, `testing`), built-in formatter (`gofmt`), test and race-detector tooling (`go test -race`).
- **Version note:** Go ships twice a year; Go 1.27.0 was announced as released on 19 August 2026, with Go 1.26 from February 2026 (checked 17 October 2026 from the release announcement and a release tracker).

Typical uses: cloud infrastructure and CLIs (Docker, Kubernetes, Terraform and many cloud tools are written in Go), network services, APIs, and DevOps tooling.

### Rust: safety and speed without a garbage collector

Rust (first stable release 2015, sponsored historically by Mozilla and now stewarded by the Rust Foundation) gives C-like performance with memory safety guaranteed at compile time. Its core idea is **ownership**: every value has one owner, and when the owner goes out of scope the value is freed; you can lend references (**borrows**), and the **borrow checker** enforces that you never have a mutable reference alongside others and never use a value after it moved or was freed. This eliminates use-after-free, double free and data races in safe code, with no runtime cost.

- **No null:** absence is `Option<T>`, failure is `Result<T, E>`, and the `?` operator propagates errors. The compiler forces you to handle each case.
- **Enums with data and pattern matching** model states precisely; **traits** provide polymorphism; **generics** are resolved at compile time (zero-cost abstractions).
- **Cargo** is a first-class build tool and package manager (`crates.io`), with built-in testing, docs and formatting (`rustfmt`, `clippy`). Language editions (2021, 2024) allow evolution without breaking old code; a new stable compiler ships every six weeks.
- **Async Rust** uses `async/await` with a runtime such as Tokio; it is powerful but has a steeper learning curve than Go's goroutines.
- **Trade-offs:** a real learning curve (fighting the borrow checker at first), longer compile times, and a smaller (but fast-growing) hiring pool.

Typical uses: systems software, browsers and engines, databases and infrastructure (many new data tools are Rust-based), WebAssembly, security-sensitive components, and high-performance Python and JavaScript tooling (Ruff, uv and others are written in Rust).

### Java and Kotlin: the enterprise backbone on the JVM

Java (1995) runs on the **Java Virtual Machine**: source compiles to bytecode, and the JVM's just-in-time compiler optimizes hot code at runtime, so long-running services reach excellent steady-state performance after a warm-up. Strengths: an enormous ecosystem (Spring and Spring Boot, Jakarta EE, Maven and Gradle, Hibernate), mature tooling and observability, strong backward compatibility, and a very large talent pool. Modern Java is far less verbose than its reputation: **records**, **sealed types**, **pattern matching**, text blocks and, since Java 21, **virtual threads** (lightweight threads that let simple blocking code scale like async code). Long-term support releases arrive roughly every two years; **Java 25** is a current LTS release (September 2025), per my background knowledge rather than a fresh check. **Garbage collectors** (G1 by default, ZGC and Shenandoah for low pause times) are tunable. **Kotlin** (JetBrains, official for Android) runs on the JVM with concise syntax, null safety and coroutines, and interoperates fully with Java. Typical uses: large backends, banking and insurance systems, Android apps (Kotlin), big-data tooling (Kafka, Spark, Elasticsearch are JVM-based).

### C# and .NET: productive, versatile, well-tooled

C# (Microsoft, 2000) runs on the cross-platform **.NET** runtime, with a JIT and optional ahead-of-time compilation (**Native AOT**). It has a strong, modern type system (records, pattern matching, nullable reference types), **`async`/`await`** (which C# pioneered and other languages adopted), **LINQ** for expressive data queries, and excellent tooling (Visual Studio, Rider, VS Code). **ASP.NET Core** is a high-performance web framework; **Entity Framework Core** is the common ORM; **Blazor** runs C# in the browser; Unity uses C# for games. .NET releases yearly, with long-term support versions on even years (.NET 10 is the LTS release from November 2025, per my background knowledge). Typical uses: enterprise backends, Azure-centered systems, Windows desktop, games (Unity), and internal business apps.

### Other languages you will meet

- **C and C++:** maximum control and performance, manual memory management, decades of libraries; used in operating systems, databases, game engines, embedded and high-frequency trading. Memory-safety bugs are the dominant source of serious vulnerabilities in large C and C++ codebases, which is why governments and large vendors now encourage memory-safe languages for new code.
- **Swift:** Apple platforms (iOS, macOS), reference counting, modern safe syntax, also usable on servers.
- **PHP and Ruby:** web-focused dynamic languages with mature frameworks (Laravel, Rails) that power a huge share of the web; still productive choices.
- **Elixir and Erlang:** the BEAM virtual machine, millions of lightweight processes and fault-tolerance by design; used for messaging and real-time systems (WhatsApp, Discord components).
- **SQL, Bash, and configuration languages (YAML, HCL):** not general-purpose, but you will write them daily.
- **Mojo, Zig, Gleam and other newer languages** are worth watching but verify maturity and ecosystem before betting a product on them.

### How to choose

1. **What does the team already know and operate?** Hiring, onboarding and on-call familiarity beat marginal language advantages.
2. **What does the ecosystem offer for this problem?** ML and data science point to Python; mobile to Kotlin and Swift; the browser to TypeScript; infrastructure tooling to Go or Rust; enterprise integration to Java or C#.
3. **What are the performance and latency needs,** measured rather than assumed? Many services are I/O-bound and any of these will do.
4. **What failure modes are unacceptable?** Memory-unsafe code in a security-critical parser argues for Rust or a managed language; very low and predictable latency argues against a heavily pause-prone GC configuration.
5. **How will it be deployed and maintained for five years?** Prefer languages with stable tooling and a healthy community. Polyglot is fine, but each new language costs training, tooling and operations.

## Worked example

The same small task in several languages makes the differences concrete. **Task:** fetch several URLs concurrently with a timeout, and report status or error per URL, without letting one slow request block the rest. First, **Go**, where goroutines, `context` and explicit errors show up:

```go
package main

import (
	"context"
	"fmt"
	"net/http"
	"sync"
	"time"
)

type result struct {
	url    string
	status int
	err    error
}

func fetch(ctx context.Context, url string) result {
	req, err := http.NewRequestWithContext(ctx, http.MethodGet, url, nil)
	if err != nil {
		return result{url: url, err: err}
	}
	resp, err := http.DefaultClient.Do(req)
	if err != nil {
		return result{url: url, err: err} // errors are values: return them, do not throw
	}
	defer resp.Body.Close()
	return result{url: url, status: resp.StatusCode}
}

func main() {
	urls := []string{"https://example.com", "https://go.dev", "https://httpbin.org/delay/5"}
	ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 2*time.Second)
	defer cancel()

	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	out := make([]result, len(urls)) // each goroutine writes its own slot: no lock needed
	for i, u := range urls {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func() { // Go 1.22+ gives each iteration its own i and u
			defer wg.Done()
			out[i] = fetch(ctx, u)
		}()
	}
	wg.Wait()
	for _, r := range out {
		if r.err != nil {
			fmt.Printf("%-30s ERROR %v\n", r.url, r.err)
		} else {
			fmt.Printf("%-30s %d\n", r.url, r.status)
		}
	}
}
```

**Rust** with Tokio (`reqwest` and `futures` crates): the compiler forces you to handle every `Result`, and ownership makes sharing explicit:

```rust
use futures::future::join_all;
use std::time::Duration;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let urls = ["https://example.com", "https://www.rust-lang.org", "https://httpbin.org/delay/5"];
    let client = reqwest::Client::builder()
        .timeout(Duration::from_secs(2)) // per-request timeout
