> [!pitfall] Choosing a language from benchmarks or fashion. Most services are I/O-bound; the database, network and architecture dominate, not the language. Pick for team skills, ecosystem and operations first, and measure before concluding that language speed is your bottleneck.
> [!pitfall] Writing Go, Rust or Java as if it were Python. Ignoring errors in Go, sprinkling `clone()` and `unwrap()` in Rust to silence the compiler, or building deep inheritance hierarchies in Java produces worse code in every ecosystem. Learn the idioms: explicit error handling and small interfaces in Go, ownership-friendly data design in Rust, records and composition in modern Java.
> [!pitfall] Underestimating the learning curve and tooling cost of a new language. A second language means new build pipelines, linters, security scanning, deployment images, on-call skills and hiring. Adopt one when the benefit is clear, start with a bounded component, and keep the interface to the rest of the system an API or queue.
> [!pitfall] Concurrency without understanding the model. Goroutines still race without synchronization (use the race detector), async code stalls if you block the event loop, and virtual or green threads do not fix an overloaded downstream service. Learn the model and add timeouts, cancellation and limits.
> [!security] Memory-unsafe languages (C and C++) account for a large share of severe vulnerabilities in big codebases, which is a main reason Rust and other memory-safe languages are encouraged for new security-critical code. Whatever the language, pin and audit dependencies (crates, Go modules, Maven packages, NuGet), use the ecosystem's vulnerability scanners, and avoid `unsafe` blocks, reflection tricks and deserialization of untrusted data.
> [!prod] Standardize the build and release path per language: a pinned toolchain version, a lockfile or module checksum, a linter and formatter in CI, reproducible container images (static binaries make tiny images), health checks and graceful shutdown, structured logging and metrics, and a runbook. JVM and .NET services need heap and GC tuning and warm-up awareness; Go and Rust services need attention to goroutine or task leaks and memory growth.
> [!cost] Language choice shows up in cloud bills through memory and CPU footprint and startup time (important for serverless): small static binaries cold-start fast and run lean, JVM and .NET services need more memory and warm-up but can be extremely efficient at steady state, and interpreted languages often need more instances for CPU-bound work. Measure on your real workload.
> [!tip] To learn a language quickly, rebuild something you already know (a small API, a CLI, the word-count program) and compare idioms against your main language, read the official tour and standard library, and use the language's formatter, linter and test tooling from day one. Notice what it makes easy and what it makes hard; that is its philosophy.
